  "worked_solution": "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=4Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8Z,3X,4Y\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta .$",
